Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design time-bound cross-account access for external partners and maintain auditability across accounts.

Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles and trust policies
- STS AssumeRole with session duration
- S3 Access Points and bucket policies per tenant
- KMS CMK rotation and envelope encryption
- CloudTrail multi-account logging and integrity
- Automated expiry and revocation mechanisms; breach simulations

Code Example

```javascript
// Example: using AWS SDK v3 to assume a role and upload to S3
import { STSClient, AssumeRoleCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-sts";
import { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-s3";

async function uploadWithTemporaryCredentials(roleArn, bucket, key, body) {
  const sts = new STSClient({ region: "us-east-1" });
  const cred = await sts.send(new AssumeRoleCommand({ RoleArn: roleArn, RoleSessionName: "partnerUpload", DurationSeconds: 900 }));
  const s3 = new S3Client({ region: "us-east-1", credentials: cred.Credentials });
  await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key, Body: body }));
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and respond to a compromised tenant session expiry breach?
- What changes would you make to support a new partner with slightly different data retention requirements?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on cross-account data access focusing on beginner-friendly, concrete controls, rotation, and rapid containment.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account roles with restricted trusts
- Least-privilege S3 and KMS usage
- CMK rotation and key policy scope
- Breach containment automation (Lambda + EventBridge)

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy for the cross-account role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ANALYTICS_ACCOUNT_ID:role/AnalyticsRole"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "customer-123"}}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Bucket policy allowing only the cross-account role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ANALYTICS_ACCOUNT_ID:role/AnalyticsRole"},
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::customer-bucket-123", "arn:aws:s3:::customer-bucket-123/*"]
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test no unintended access (rotation, revocation, logs)?
- What monitoring alerts would you wire to detect anomalous cross-account access?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of least privilege, data security in transit/at rest, cross-account auditing, and implementing simple defense-in-depth for a common ingestion pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Presigned URLs, Lambda IAM, S3 bucket policies
- SSE-KMS and CMK rotation
- S3 Object Lock and immutable logs
- CloudTrail integration
- Input validation (Content-Type, size)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Lambda presigned URL generator outline
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const {filename, contentType, size}=JSON.parse(event.body);
  if (!contentType.startsWith('image/') || size>5*1024*1024) throw new Error('Invalid');
  const url = s3.getSignedUrl('putObject', {Bucket:'image-upload-bucket',Key:filename,ContentType:contentType,Expires:300});
  return {statusCode:200, body: JSON.stringify({url})};
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate the CMK and rotate keys used in the bucket policy without breaking existing uploads?
- What logging verifications would you run to confirm immutability and no unauthorized uses?

